-- speech: `--long-form` speaks a long Drama 3 input in segments of up to `--long-form-chars` (default 300). Each segment hears the earlier segments' text and generated codes as references (`--long-form-history`, default 1), so prosody carries across the joins. At each join only the pause the punctuation asks for and the model left out is added, as room tone (0.42 s after `.` and `!` up to 0.70 s for a paragraph), and a segment off the earlier segments' speech level gets one gain of at most 3 dB. A reply has at most 12 segments and 6000 frames. `X-Long-Form-Segments` gives where each segment starts, and streams send the segments in order. With the flag off, or an input that fits in one segment, replies are unchanged ([docs](docs/drama-voices.md#long-form-replies))
+- speech: `--long-form` speaks a long Drama 3 input in the fewest segments of up to `--long-form-chars` (default 500), balanced in length and cut where the text turns (a question, or a sentence opening with So, Okay, Now, Anyway, Here's, …), with a 0.60 s pause at such a cut. Each segment hears the earlier segments' text and generated codes as references (`--long-form-history`, default 1), so prosody carries across the joins. At each join only the pause the punctuation asks for and the model left out is added, as room tone (0.42 s after `.` and `!`, up to 0.70 s for a paragraph), and a segment off the earlier segments' speech level gets one gain of at most 3 dB. A reply has at most 12 segments and 6000 frames. `X-Long-Form-Segments` gives where each segment starts, and streams send the segments in order. With the flag off, or an input that fits in one segment, replies are unchanged ([docs](docs/drama-voices.md#long-form-replies))
 - speech: Drama 3 speaks in a reference voice. A request can send `references: [{text, vq_codes}]`. `--voices DIR` loads voice files a request names with `voice`, and `--default-voice NAME` uses one when a request names no voice. `tools/zig/drama_voice.py` encodes a clip offline with the pinned reference's codec encoder. The prompt rows match the reference's `build_prompt_rows` (fixture-gated with the real tokenizer), and requests without a voice reference build the same prompt as before ([docs](docs/drama-voices.md))
 - speech: `stream: true` with `response_format: pcm` streams HTTP/1.1 chunks while Drama 3 generates; incremental codec decode bit-identical to the whole decode (oracle-gated)
 - speech: Drama 3 generates 1.66x faster on a GB10 (warm real-time factor 1.087 to 0.655 over the 23 warm items of a 48-request capture), with the audio of every normally finished reply byte for byte the same. The one-row backbone GEMVs stream their weights through a swizzled `cp.async` ring, add-norm, SwiGLU, MoE combine and attention glue are fused or pipelined in the same float order, the tower runs its one-row decode attention as a cluster kernel and samples with a radix select, and the backbone chain launches with programmatic dependent launch when both embedded kernel images carry the `drama_pdl` marker. The tower's one-row projections run on `drama_tower_gemv`, which reproduces cuBLASLt 130101's gemv summation; `Model.init` checks each projection against its actual cuBLASLt plan on probe rows and keeps cuBLASLt for any projection that differs by one bit, or on any other GPU or library.
